---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";
---

<Base
  title="Support"
  description="Get help with GibTalk: contact, backups, templates and speech."
>
  <h1>Support</h1>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>. Tell
    me which phone or tablet you use and what happened, and I'll get back to
    you.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have a GitHub account, you can also
    <a href={SITE.issues}>open an issue on GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <p>
    For step-by-step help with everything in the app, see the
    <a href="/manual/">user manual</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>How do I add or change words?</h3>
  <p>
    Tap <strong>Edit</strong> and type the four numbers shown. This keeps
    children from changing words by accident. In edit mode, tap
    <strong>Add</strong> for a new word, tap the pencil on a word to change it,
    and press and hold a word to drag it somewhere else. Tap
    <strong>Done</strong> when you're finished.
    <a href="/manual/#edit-mode">More in the manual</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>How do I back up my words?</h3>
  <p>
    In edit mode, tap <strong>More → Backup</strong>. GibTalk makes a file with
    all your words and pictures and opens the share menu, so you can save it to
    your files or send it to yourself.
  </p>

  <h3>How do I move my words to a new device?</h3>
  <p>
    Make a backup on the old device and get the file onto the new one. Then, in
    edit mode, tap <strong>More → Restore</strong> and pick the file.
    <strong>Restore replaces all the words on the device</strong>, so back up
    first if there's anything there you want to keep.
  </p>

  <h3>What are templates?</h3>
  <p>
    Ready-made sets of words. In edit mode, tap
    <strong>More → Templates</strong> and pick one. A template is added to your
    words as a new folder at the end of Home; it doesn't replace them. You can
    look through them in the
    <a href="/builder/">template builder</a> (Start from a template…).
  </p>
  <p>
    You can also make your own word set on a computer with the
    <a href="/builder/">template builder</a>, and download it as a file. To add
    it to GibTalk, use <strong>Import from file</strong> at the top of the
    Templates screen: it adds the words and keeps the ones you have.
    <strong>Restore</strong> also opens the file, but replaces all your words
    with it.
  </p>

  <h3>A word doesn't speak, or speaks in the wrong voice</h3>
  <p>
    GibTalk uses your device's text-to-speech. If a language doesn't speak, its
    voice may not be installed. GibTalk usually notices and offers
    <strong>Install voice</strong>; otherwise:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Android:</strong> Settings → search for "Text-to-speech output" →
      open the settings of the preferred engine → install voice data for the
      language.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>iPhone and iPad:</strong> Settings → Accessibility → Spoken
      Content → Voices, and download a voice for the language.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Also check that the word's language is set correctly: in edit mode, tap the
    pencil on the word and look at its language. And write the word in that
    language's own script: a Tamil voice reads அம்மா, not "Amma".
    <a href="/manual/#languages">More about languages in the manual</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Where do the pictures come from?</h3>
  <p>
    Symbol search uses free symbol libraries (ARASAAC, Mulberry and Tawasol).
    You can also take a photo or pick one from your device.
    <a href="/symbols/#credits">Symbol credits</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Does GibTalk collect my data?</h3>
  <p>
    No. There's no account, no ads and no analytics, and your words stay on your
    device. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
